select public.prepify_load_bank('Chemistry', 5, $j$[["What is the primary driving force for atoms to form chemical bonds?",["To increase their total energy.","To become radioactive.","To achieve a stable noble gas electronic configuration.","To increase the number of protons."],"C",3,0,"74a0366eda77"],["An atom forms three covalent bonds. It most likely needs:",["Three electrons for stability","Two electrons for stability","One electron for stability","Four electrons for stability"],"A",3,0,"61d6c77c6e09"],["Which statement best explains the formation of ionic compounds?",["Like charges attract each other","Oppositely charged ions attract each other","Neutrons attract electrons","Protons are exchanged"],"B",3,0,"6108496c5788"],["Which rule states that atoms tend to gain, lose, or share electrons to have eight electrons in their valence shell?",["Pauli exclusion principle","Duplet rule","Hund’s rule","Octet rule"],"D",3,0,"b7ab9e0afc93"],["Which of the following is a characteristic property of ionic compounds?",["Low melting points.","High electrical conductivity in the solid state.","High melting points and brittleness.","Solubility only in organic solvents."],"C",3,0,"91279ee2f9d0"],["Which type of bond is responsible for the high electrical and thermal conductivity of metals?",["Covalent bond.","Metallic bond","Hydrogen bond.","Ionic bond."],"B",3,0,"dd2a1b6ad7ab"],["What determines the strength of an ionic bond?",["The number of neutrons.","The color of the crystal.","The magnitude of the charges on the ions and the distance between them.","The atomic mass of the elements."],"C",3,0,"b72a9bcd0ec5"],["In the formation of a covalent bond, what happens to the energy of the system?",["It disappears.","It remains unchanged.","It increases.","It decreases to reach a stable energy minimum."],"D",3,0,"9259b9fa56be"],["Electropositive elements are typically:",["Metals","Noble gases","Non-metals.","Metalloids"],"A",3,0,"3f0e2e3a6f91"],["Which substance would have the highest melting point?",["CH₄","NaCl","H₂O","O₂"],"B",3,0,"4cfa2d345abb"],["Covalent compounds usually have:",["A rigid lattice structure.","Ionic conductivity.","High boiling points.","Low boiling points due to weak intermolecular forces between molecules."],"D",3,0,"da337f7ab047"],["Atoms with high electronegativity are more likely to form:",["Cations.","Metallic bonds.","Anions.","Nothing."],"C",3,0,"8d83b7ef4865"],["In the formation of MgO, what charge do the ions acquire?",["+1, -1","+2, -2","+1, -2","0, 0"],"B",3,0,"7ecd757c1eb9"],["What represents the \"cloud\" in metallic bonding?",["The neutrons.","The empty orbitals.","The protons.","The valence electrons that are no longer associated with a single nucleus."],"D",3,0,"a7a5abc748fb"],["Why is it that covalent bonds are generally strong?",["Because shared electrons are held tightly by two positively charged nuclei.","Because they involve transfer.","Because they are large.","Because they only occur in solids."],"A",3,0,"fca229843def"],["Which of the following substances would conduct electricity as a solid?",["NaCl","Copper","Diamond","H₂O"],"B",3,0,"bc3218a1f372"],["Why is fluorine highly reactive?",["It has no valence electrons","It easily loses seven electrons","It has a complete octet","It needs one electron to complete its octet"],"D",3,0,"f4f1ba75a65f"],["Why is electron transfer unlikely between two oxygen atoms?",["Both atoms tend to gain electrons rather than lose them","Oxygen has no valence electrons","Oxygen atoms are metals","Oxygen atoms already have complete octets"],"A",3,0,"8c362c283451"],["A student says, “Every bond involves electron transfer.” Which substance proves the statement incorrect?",["NaCl","MgO","H₂O","KCl"],"C",3,1,"a4b20da7c9ba"],["Why does hydrogen form one covalent bond, but oxygen usually forms two?",["Oxygen is heavier than hydrogen","Hydrogen needs one electron, and oxygen needs two electrons for stability","Oxygen has fewer electrons","Hydrogen forms only ionic bonds"],"B",3,0,"536db92cc178"],["Why does the melting point of magnesium oxide (MgO) significantly exceed that of sodium chloride (NaCl)?",["The lattice energy of MgO is much higher due to the greater electrostatic attraction between +2/-2 ions compared to +1/-1 ions.","MgO is a covalent compound.","NaCl is a metal.","Mg and O are smaller than Na and Cl."],"A",3,0,"d7f45cfc8339"],["In the molecule BF₃, the Boron atom is said to be \"electron-deficient.\" What does this mean?",["It has 8 valence electrons.","It has only 6 valence electrons, meaning it does not satisfy the octet rule.","It has 10 valence electrons.","It is a metal."],"B",3,0,"2d4baa6b00b3"],["When an atom forms a covalent bond, what is happening to the atomic orbitals?",["They expand to infinite size.","They are destroyed.","They become ionic.","They overlap to create a region of space where electrons are likely to be shared."],"D",3,0,"8c19d73fcd23"],["Which of these is a non-directional force?",["The covalent bond between C and H.","The metallic bond between Na atoms.","The electrostatic attraction in an ionic lattice.","Both B and C."],"D",3,0,"b4ab0e483eed"],["What happens to the electronegativity difference as you go from an ionic to a covalent bond?",["It becomes negative.","It increases.","It stays the same.","It decreases."],"D",3,0,"43d866ec4bfa"],["An atom X loses one electron while atom Y gains one electron. Which statement is correct?",["Both become negatively charged","X becomes a cation, and Y becomes an anion","Both become positively charged","X becomes an anion, and Y becomes a cation"],"B",3,0,"f6a07233a9e9"],["You are given an unknown substance X. It has a very high melting point, is brittle, and does not conduct electricity as a solid. When you dissolve it in water, the solution conducts electricity vigorously. What is the nature of X?",["Polar covalent molecule","Metallic solid","Covalent network solid","Ionic lattice"],"D",3,0,"5ac5eeed921a"],["A scientist discovers a material that conducts electricity in both the solid and liquid states and is highly malleable. What type of bonding is present?",["Covalent","Metallic","Ionic","Dative"],"B",3,1,"7e5a2650cc7c"],["A student compares NaCl and Cl₂. Both contain chlorine atoms. Why is chlorine present as ions in one substance but as molecules in the other?",["Chlorine changes its atomic number","The bonding partner is different","Chlorine gains protons in NaCl","Chlorine loses neutrons in Cl₂"],"B",3,1,"914c7bf30e2c"],["A metal atom loses two electrons while a non-metal atom gains two electrons. What is the ratio of ions in the resulting compound?",["1 : 1","1 : 2","2 : 1","2 : 3"],"A",3,0,"93fc29a0ecaf"],["You have two liquids: H₂O and CCl₄. You notice that NaCl dissolves in H₂O but not in CCl₄. Why?",["CCl₄ is too viscous.","NaCl is covalent.","H₂O is polar and interacts with ions; CCl₄ is non-polar and cannot stabilize ions.","H₂O is a metal."],"C",3,0,"af5fd09a30ac"],["An atom Z has an empty 2p orbital and forms a bond with NH₃, where both electrons are provided by the Nitrogen. What is this bond called?",["Coordinate covalent bond","Ionic bond","Metallic bond","Hydrogen bond"],"A",3,0,"aeaf06fcf0f4"],["You add a substance to a beaker of water; the substance sinks and does not dissolve, and it does not conduct electricity. What kind of substance is it likely to be?",["A salt","An ionic compound","A metal","A simple covalent molecule"],"D",3,0,"1763e04e108e"],["A scientist labels a bond as \"non-directional.\" Which type of bonding could this be?",["Ionic or Metallic.","Covalent only.","Only metallic.","Only ionic."],"A",3,1,"ad3d60c57519"],["Why do ionic crystals shatter when struck by a hammer?",["They become covalent.","The layers slide, and like-charged ions end up adjacent to each other, causing massive repulsion.","The electrons escape.","The metal atoms bend."],"B",3,0,"df36ac2c19e0"],["Why can't a simple covalent molecule like CH₄ conduct electricity?",["It has no ions and no delocalized electrons.","It is too small.","It has no protons.","It is metallic."],"A",3,0,"1bd02de2241a"],["If an atom of Magnesium reacts with Chlorine, what happens to the energy level?",["Energy remains constant.","The atom disappears.","Energy is absorbed.","Energy is released as the system reaches a more stable configuration."],"D",3,1,"0328a33f08c9"],["Why are ionic compounds solids at room temperature?",["They have weak bonds.","The electrostatic forces between ions are so strong that they lock them into a rigid, repeating lattice.","They are metals.","They have no bonds."],"B",3,0,"d2eaea25553a"],["You find a substance that is a \"giant\" structure, very hard, and does not conduct electricity. What could it be?",["Graphite","Sodium Chloride","Silicon Dioxide","Copper"],"C",3,0,"74d4f804d78a"],["Why are delocalized electrons in metals considered \"delocalized\"?",["They are not bound to any single atom and can move throughout the lattice.","They are lost.","They are in the nucleus.","They are in the inner shell."],"A",3,0,"4f3cd043e374"],["An atom with six valence electrons reacts with hydrogen atoms. How many covalent bonds will it most likely form?",["One","Two","Three","Four"],"B",3,0,"894b8d38263b"],["A student notices that an ionic compound remains electrically neutral despite containing charged particles. Why?",["Positive and negative charges balance each other","Electrons disappear during bonding","Only positive ions are present","Only negative ions are present"],"A",3,1,"2cdf433508a2"],["A student argues that atoms bond because they want to increase their mass. Which observation best refutes this idea?",["Bonding increases neutrons","Bonding creates new elements","Bonding increases the atomic number","Bonding changes electron arrangement, not nuclear composition"],"D",3,1,"f83de9f9942f"],["An atom contributes both electrons in a shared pair while the other contributes none. Before bond formation, the second atom most likely had:",["No valence electrons","A vacant orbital available for the pair","Excess neutrons","A positive nucleus only"],"B",3,0,"3632f5579bb0"],["A student observes that chlorine forms Cl⁻ rather than Cl⁺ during ionic bond formation. The most reasonable explanation is that chlorine:",["Easily loses seven electrons","Is a metal","Has one valence electron","Needs one electron for stability"],"D",3,1,"528ed64e81af"],["An atom gains electrons during bond formation. Which quantity remains unchanged?",["Electronic configuration","Atomic number","Charge","Number of electrons"],"B",3,0,"93c7d8231a37"],["Element P has 2 valence electrons, and Element Q has 7 valence electrons. If they react, what is the most likely formula and bond type?",["PQ₂, Covalent","P₂Q, Ionic","PQ₂, Ionic","PQ₇, Metallic"],"C",3,0,"86cff65a999f"],["Why does the bond angle in NH₃ (107o) differ from the ideal tetrahedral angle (109.5o)?",["The lone pair on Nitrogen exerts more repulsion on the bonding pairs than the bonding pairs do on each other.","NH₃ is an ionic compound.","Nitrogen has too many protons.","H atoms are too large."],"A",3,0,"c96fc33c307d"],["Which of the following explains why NH₃ has a higher boiling point than PH₃?",["NH₃ has stronger Van der Waals forces.","Nitrogen is more electronegative than Phosphorus, allowing NH₃ to form intermolecular Hydrogen bonds, while PH₃ cannot.","NH₃ is a larger molecule.","PH₃is ionic."],"B",3,0,"10665581ffa0"],["In the molecule SF₆, the Sulfur atom is said to have an \"expanded octet.\" How is this physically possible?",["It is an ionic compound.","It has no bonds.","Sulfur uses its vacant 3d orbitals to accommodate more than 8 valence electrons.","It loses its nucleus."],"C",3,0,"d89348e42ae7"],["Which factor is the most significant in determining the \"covalent character\" of an ionic bond?",["High charge and small size of the cation.","Large size of the cation.","Low electronegativity of the anion.","The color of the crystal."],"A",3,0,"3f3364c33bca"],["Which of the following bonds is the most polar?",["C-H","C-F","C-O","C-N"],"B",3,0,"37aefda6494f"],["Why is the bond energy of O₂ (double bond) not exactly twice the bond energy of O-O (single bond)?",["Double bonds are always exactly double.","O₂ is ionic.","It depends on the color.","π-bonds are generally weaker than σ-bonds due to less effective orbital overlap, so they do not add linearly."],"D",3,0,"b13b840901bf"],["Which of the following ions is most likely to show the highest degree of polarization in a bond?",["Na⁺","Al³⁺","K⁺","Ca²⁺"],"B",3,0,"b8b344a6b208"],["If electrons in a metallic bond were localized between specific atoms, metals would most likely lose their:",["Color","Malleability","Atomic mass","Density"],"B",3,0,"b950aa602235"],["In an ionic crystal, each ion is surrounded by ions of opposite charge. This arrangement primarily serves to:",["Reduce atomic number.","Increase neutron count.","Maximize electrostatic stability.","Form molecules."],"C",3,0,"05f8c54966dc"],["In the ammonium ion, NH₃ donates both electrons of a bond to H⁺. This bond is a:",["coordinate covalent bond","ionic bond","metallic bond","hydrogen bond"],"A",3,0,"aeaf06fcf0f4"],["Atoms tend to gain, lose or share electrons so that their outer shell holds eight electrons. This is the:",["duplet rule","octet rule","Hund's rule","Aufbau rule"],"B",1,0,"b7ab9e0afc93"],["Which substance conducts electricity in the solid state?",["Sugar","Aluminium","Sulphur","Ice"],"B",3,0,"bc3218a1f372"],["Element X has 1 valence electron and element Y has 6. The compound they form is most likely:",["XY₂, covalent","X₂Y, ionic","XY, covalent","XY₆, ionic"],"B",5,0,"86cff65a999f"],["Which bond is the most polar?",["H–H","H–Cl","H–F","Cl–Cl"],"C",5,0,"37aefda6494f"],["A white solid has a high melting point, does not conduct as a solid, but conducts when dissolved in water. It is most likely:",["an ionic compound","a metal","a covalent gas","graphite"],"A",3,1,"5ac5eeed921a"],["Nitrogen usually forms three covalent bonds because it:",["needs three more electrons to complete its octet","has three shells","is a metal","has no valence electrons"],"A",3,0,"536db92cc178"],["A covalent bond forms when the orbitals of two atoms:",["overlap and share a pair of electrons","move apart","transfer all electrons","become ions"],"A",3,0,"8c19d73fcd23"],["The formula of the compound formed between sodium ions (Na⁺) and fluoride ions (F⁻) is:",["Na₂F₃","Na₃F₂","NaF","Na₂F"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c5-ionic-formula"],["The formula of the compound formed between potassium ions (K⁺) and nitride ions (N³⁻) is:",["KN₃","K₃N","KN","K₂N₃"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c5-ionic-formula"],["The formula of the compound formed between aluminium ions (Al³⁺) and oxide ions (O²⁻) is:",["Al₃O₂","Al₂O₃","AlO","Al₂O"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c5-ionic-formula"],["The formula of the compound formed between magnesium ions (Mg²⁺) and nitride ions (N³⁻) is:",["MgN","Mg₃N₂","Mg₂N₃","Mg₃N"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c5-ionic-formula"],["The formula of the compound formed between magnesium ions (Mg²⁺) and oxide ions (O²⁻) is:",["Mg₂O₂","MgO","Mg₂O","MgO₂"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c5-ionic-formula"],["The formula of the compound formed between magnesium ions (Mg²⁺) and fluoride ions (F⁻) is:",["Mg₂F","MgF","Mg₂F₃","MgF₂"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c5-ionic-formula"],["How many shared pairs of electrons are there in a molecule of N₂?",["4","3","6","2"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c5-shared-pairs"],["How many shared pairs of electrons are there in a molecule of CH₄?",["5","8","4","3"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c5-shared-pairs"],["How many shared pairs of electrons are there in a molecule of CO₂?",["5","3","8","4"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c5-shared-pairs"],["How many shared pairs of electrons are there in a molecule of HCl?",["4","3","1","2"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c5-shared-pairs"],["How many shared pairs of electrons are there in a molecule of H₂O?",["1","2","3","4"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c5-shared-pairs"],["How many shared pairs of electrons are there in a molecule of C₂H₄?",["12","7","5","6"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c5-shared-pairs"]]$j$::jsonb, true);
select public.prepify_load_bank('Chemistry', 6, $j$[["How many particles are in one mole of a substance?",["6.022 x 10²³","6.022 x 10²²","1.66 x 10⁻²⁴","6.022 x10¹³"],"A",3,0,"c727d91f6d11"],["What is the relationship between mass, moles, and molar mass?",["Moles = Mass x Molar Mass","Molar Mass = Mass x Moles","Mass = Moles x Molar Mass","Mass = Moles / Molar Mass"],"C",3,0,"eac8f1ecde64"],["What does a chemical equation with state symbols represent?",["Only the reactants.","The stoichiometry of the reaction and the physical state of all components.","The time taken for the reaction.","Only the products."],"B",3,0,"8d26f9f40e8e"],["In a chemical equation, what do the coefficients indicate?",["The number of grams.","The molar mass.","The number of moles reacting.","The number of atoms."],"C",3,0,"580c503093ad"],["A binary compound is formed from ions Na⁺ and Cl⁻. What is its formula?",["Na₂Cl","NaCl","Na₂Cl₂","NaCl₂"],"B",3,0,"7ed879e900cb"],["What is the simplest formula of a molecule containing C₆H₁₂O₆?",["C₆H₁₂O₆","C₂H₄O₂","C₃H₆O₃","CH₂O"],"D",3,0,"61b0efb3eafe"],["If you have 0.5 moles of O₂, how many molecules of O₂ do you have?",["6.022×10²³","3.011×10²³","0.5","1.204×10²⁴"],"B",3,0,"8677b0e49163"],["What does the formula of a binary compound like MgCl₂ tell us?",["It has 2 Magnesium atoms.","Magnesium has a -2 charge.","It is a covalent molecule.","It has a ratio of 1 Magnesium ion to 2 Chloride ions."],"D",3,0,"a301086574fc"],["Which is a reactant in the following equation? C+O₂→CO₂",["CO₂","C and O₂","O₂","C"],"B",3,0,"f63db279270c"],["Which of the following equations is balanced?",["N₂+H₂→NH₃","N₂+H₂→2NH₃","N₂+3H₂→2NH₃","2N₂+H₂→NH₃"],"C",3,0,"027e8584a8b4"],["What does the state symbol \"(s)\" indicate in a chemical equation?",["The substance is in a solid state.","The substance is dissolved in water.","The substance is in a liquid state.","The substance is a solution."],"A",3,0,"4d2a8c4e79f1"],["Why is the mole considered a \"bridge\" in stoichiometry?",["It counts electrons only.","It is used to balance ionic charges.","It connects the microscopic world of atoms/molecules to the macroscopic world of grams.","It is the only unit that measures volume."],"C",3,0,"bb6319f602a7"],["If you double the amount of moles of a reactant in a balanced equation, what happens to the theoretical amount of product?",["It stays the same.","It doubles.","It is halved.","It becomes zero."],"B",3,0,"93f70649d3eb"],["How does the Law of Conservation of Mass dictate chemical equation balancing?",["Atoms must be created to balance charge.","Mass can be destroyed if the energy is high enough.","Only the products have mass.","The total mass of reactants must equal the total mass of products, requiring the same number of each type of atom on both sides."],"D",3,0,"c72d0bc67487"],["Which of these contains the largest number of atoms?",["1 mole of H₂O","1 mole of NaCl","1 mole of CH₄","1 mole of CO₂"],"C",3,0,"f0b647fe306a"],["If a reaction equation is A+2B→C, and you have 2 moles of A and 2 moles of B, which is the limiting reactant?",["A","B","Both are consumed equally.","C"],"B",3,1,"cad7b8676669"],["How many moles of Hydrogen atoms are in 0.5 moles of NH₃?",["0.5","1.0","1.5","3.0"],"C",3,0,"8de74113c845"],["Which measurement is not necessary to find the empirical formula of a compound?",["The mass percentage of each element.","The atomic masses of the elements.","The ratio of the moles of elements.","The molar mass of the compound."],"D",3,0,"e0d780ac9561"],["What does a molar mass of 18.02 g/mol tell you physically?",["That 1 molecule weighs 18.02g.","That the element is a solid.","That 6.022×10²³molecules weigh 18.02 g.","That 1 atom weighs 18.02 g."],"C",3,0,"69d83d1c4130"],["How many grams of Carbon are in 1 mole of Carbon?",["1g","12g","6g","24g"],"B",3,0,"f5bb5382b15b"],["How many atoms are in one molecule of H₂SO₄?",["3","6","7","4"],"C",3,0,"82dd2662e8a0"],["What is the empirical formula of a compound with 40% Carbon, 6.7% Hydrogen, and 53.3% Oxygen?",["CH₂O","C₂H₄O₂","CHO","C₃H₆O₃"],"A",3,0,"cfca94318929"],["Why can't we determine the molecular formula from just the empirical formula?",["Because the elements are different.","Because the molar mass of the actual compound might be a multiple of the empirical formula's mass.","Because empirical formulas don't use real numbers.","Because ratios are wrong."],"B",3,0,"bace7e7af05a"],["Why is the \"mole\" a better unit than \"gram\" for chemical reactions?",["Moles represent the actual count of reactive units, whereas grams depend on atomic mass.","Grams are too small.","Moles are easier to measure.","Grams are not used in science."],"A",3,0,"00deff44c2e5"],["Why is an empirical formula used for ionic compounds?",["Because they are too heavy.","Because they have no ratio.","Because they are covalent.","Because they form infinite lattices, not discrete molecules."],"D",3,0,"488dfde051a5"],["You have a compound with the empirical formula CH₂. If the molar mass of the actual molecular compound is 84 g/mol, what is the molecular formula?",["C₂H₄","C₄H₈","C₆H₁₂","C₃H₆"],"C",3,0,"b60f5ea245a9"],["In the combustion of CH₄ (CH₄+2O₂→CO₂+2H₂O), if you have 1 mole of each reactant, which is the limiting reactant and why?",["CH₄, because it has fewer moles.","O₂, because the stoichiometric ratio requires 2 moles of O₂for every 1 mole of CH₄.","CH₄, because it is heavier.","O₂, because it is a gas."],"B",3,0,"e4cf50f8d319"],["Why do we use ionic equations instead of full molecular equations in aqueous reactions?",["Because state symbols are not used in ionic equations.","To save ink.","Because molecular equations are always wrong.","To highlight the specific ions that undergo chemical change, ignoring spectator ions."],"D",3,0,"466b33793146"],["You are performing a synthesis where the balanced equation is A+3B→2C. You mix 4 moles of A with 9 moles of B. After the reaction goes to completion, which species are present in the beaker?",["Only product C","Product C and excess reactant B","Product C and excess reactant A","Only reactant B"],"C",3,0,"47a8d944fdc4"],["You have two beakers. Beaker 1 contains 1mol of NaCl. Beaker 2 contains 1mol of CaCl₂. If you add excess AgNO₃ to both to precipitate AgCl, which beaker produces more precipitate, and by what factor?",["Beaker 1; 1:1 ratio.","Beaker 2; produces twice as much AgCl because it has 2 moles of Cl⁻ ions per mole of compound.","Beaker 2; produces 3 times as much.","Beaker 1; produces twice as much."],"B",3,0,"a4ab9aae95e8"],["Why is it impossible to determine the molecular formula of a hydrocarbon knowing only the percentage composition by mass?",["Because percentage composition only gives the ratio, not the total molar mass required for the molecular formula.","Because mass percentages are always wrong.","Because hydrocarbons don't have molecular formulas.","Because the molar mass of Carbon is variable."],"A",3,0,"7a95a476e0a9"],["You have a sample of H₂O and D₂O (Heavy water). They have the same number of moles, but different masses. Why?",["D₂O is a gas.","D₂O has more electrons.","H₂O Is ionic.","D₂O has more neutrons."],"D",3,0,"4563b7178c05"],["What does the coefficient \"3\" in 3O₂ represent in a chemical equation?",["3 grams of oxygen.","3 moles of O₂ molecules.","3 atoms of oxygen.","3 moles of oxygen atoms."],"B",3,0,"fea52515363a"],["Why is the atomic mass of Carbon 12.01 and not exactly 12?",["Measurement errors.","Electrons have mass.","It includes isotopes like C-13 and C-14.","Carbon is an ion."],"C",3,0,"97690146ac1a"],["Why is the equation H₂ + O₂ → H₂O considered incorrect?",["Hydrogen is a metal","Oxygen should be monatomic","Water cannot be formed","Atoms are not balanced"],"D",3,0,"2f1ba227f69a"],["Which balanced equation is correct?",["Fe + O₂ → Fe₂O₃","4Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃","2Fe + O₂ → Fe₂O₃","2Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃"],"B",3,0,"cecbcc205b6a"],["Which change would NOT affect the validity of a balanced equation?",["Multiplying all coefficients by the same number","Removing coefficients","Changing subscripts","Changing formulas"],"A",3,0,"ab6eeda50741"],["A student calculates the molar mass of CaCO₃ as 100 g/mol. Which step is essential in this calculation?",["Using atomic numbers","Multiplying by Avogadro’s number","Adding the relative atomic masses of all atoms in the formula","Dividing by the number of atoms"],"C",3,1,"e85a49772572"],["During a reaction, the total number of molecules changes, but the equation remains balanced. Which law is still obeyed?",["Conservation of energy","Boyle’s law","Charles’s law","Conservation of mass"],"D",3,1,"19ff702f2b5e"],["When balancing equations, why is it often useful to balance oxygen and hydrogen atoms last?",["They are usually monatomic.","They often appear in multiple compounds and are easier to adjust later.","They never affect the coefficients of other substances.","Their coefficients are always one."],"B",3,0,"e1e278026019"],["A compound contains calcium and nitrogen. Calcium forms Ca²⁺ ions, and nitrogen forms N³⁻ ions. What formula will the compound have?",["CaN","Ca₂N₃","CaN₂","Ca₃N₂"],"D",3,0,"b998b56703d9"],["While solving a stoichiometry problem, a student converts mass to moles before using the balanced equation. Why is this step necessary?",["Balanced equations relate particles and moles, not masses directly.","Moles are always equal to grams.","Mass is conserved only after conversion.","Avogadro’s number cannot be used with grams."],"A",3,1,"b0dbeb28af18"],["A student notices that ionic equations include aqueous ions but omit solid precipitates. Why?",["Solids do not dissociate into ions in solution.","Solids do not participate in reactions.","Solids have no charge.","Solids are always spectator ions."],"A",3,1,"08b0296256fa"],["A chemist needs to prepare a neutral compound from Al³⁺ and SO₄²⁻ ions. Which formula is correct?",["AlSO₄","Al₂(SO₄)₃","Al₃SO₄","Al(SO₄)₂"],"B",3,1,"c84c4b1b9fe2"],["A student compares one mole of oxygen gas with one mole of oxygen atoms. Which statement is correct?",["Both contain the same number of atoms.","Oxygen atoms contain twice as many molecules.","Both have different Avogadro numbers.","Oxygen gas contains twice as many oxygen atoms."],"D",3,1,"1182b7baec33"],["If a reaction has a theoretical yield of 50g and an actual yield of 40g, what is the percentage yield, and what does this imply?",["80%; it implies incomplete reaction or losses during separation.","20%; it implies the reaction is efficient.","100%; yield is always theoretical.","40%; it implies the reaction was too fast."],"A",3,1,"6996c937b647"],["A chemist writes the molecular equation for a reaction, but the net ionic equation is much shorter. Why?",["Molecular equations are always incorrect.","Net ionic equations ignore charges.","Molecular equations contain only solids.","Net ionic equations include only substances that actually change during the reaction."],"D",3,1,"e50a69b42f22"],["Which statement best explains why coefficients are used instead of changing subscripts when balancing equations?",["Subscripts represent fixed chemical identities of substances","Coefficients cannot be changed","Subscripts represent masses only","Coefficients change the formula"],"A",3,0,"5571e858e604"],["The molar mass of MgO is: (Mg = 24, O = 16)",["40 g/mol","38 g/mol","56 g/mol","80 g/mol"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-molar-mass"],["The molar mass of CaCO₃ is: (Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)",["98 g/mol","116 g/mol","68 g/mol","100 g/mol"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-molar-mass"],["The molar mass of HCl is: (H = 1, Cl = 35.5)",["52.5 g/mol","73 g/mol","36.5 g/mol","34.5 g/mol"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-molar-mass"],["The molar mass of CH₄ is: (C = 12, H = 1)",["13 g/mol","14 g/mol","32 g/mol","16 g/mol"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-molar-mass"],["The molar mass of NaOH is: (Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1)",["80 g/mol","56 g/mol","38 g/mol","40 g/mol"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-molar-mass"],["The molar mass of HNO₃ is: (H = 1, N = 14, O = 16)",["31 g/mol","63 g/mol","79 g/mol","61 g/mol"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-molar-mass"],["How many moles are present in 196 g of H₂SO₄? (molar mass = 98 g/mol)",["4 mol","2 mol","0.5 mol","19,208 mol"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-moles-from-mass"],["How many moles are present in 168 g of KOH? (molar mass = 56 g/mol)",["0.33 mol","6 mol","9408 mol","3 mol"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-moles-from-mass"],["How many moles are present in 300 g of CaCO₃? (molar mass = 100 g/mol)",["30,000 mol","0.33 mol","6 mol","3 mol"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-moles-from-mass"],["How many moles are present in 73 g of HCl? (molar mass = 36.5 g/mol)",["2664.5 mol","4 mol","0.5 mol","2 mol"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-moles-from-mass"],["How many moles are present in 85 g of NH₃? (molar mass = 17 g/mol)",["5 mol","1445 mol","10 mol","0.2 mol"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-moles-from-mass"],["How many moles are present in 120 g of NaOH? (molar mass = 40 g/mol)",["6 mol","0.33 mol","3 mol","4800 mol"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-moles-from-mass"],["What is the mass of 0.1 mol of MgO? (molar mass = 40 g/mol)",["400 g","40 g","4 g","0.0025 g"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-mass-from-moles"],["What is the mass of 0.2 mol of NaOH? (molar mass = 40 g/mol)",["8 g","40 g","200 g","0.005 g"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-mass-from-moles"],["What is the mass of 0.2 mol of HNO₃? (molar mass = 63 g/mol)",["63 g","0.0032 g","12.6 g","315 g"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-mass-from-moles"],["What is the mass of 1.5 mol of HNO₃? (molar mass = 63 g/mol)",["63 g","94.5 g","0.0238 g","42 g"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-mass-from-moles"],["What is the mass of 1.5 mol of CO₂? (molar mass = 44 g/mol)",["29.33 g","44 g","0.0341 g","66 g"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-mass-from-moles"],["What is the mass of 1.5 mol of C₆H₁₂O₆? (molar mass = 180 g/mol)",["180 g","270 g","0.0083 g","120 g"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-mass-from-moles"],["How many atoms in total are present in 1.5 mol of H₂O? (Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)",["1.806 × 10²⁴","9.03 × 10²³","2.709 × 10²⁴","2.709 × 10²⁵"],"C",3,0,"8677b0e49163"],["How many atoms in total are present in 5 mol of H₂O? (Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)",["3.01 × 10²⁴","9.03 × 10²⁴","9.03 × 10²⁵","1.806 × 10²⁴"],"B",3,0,"8677b0e49163"],["How many atoms in total are present in 5 mol of N₂? (Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)",["6.02 × 10²⁵","1.204 × 10²⁴","3.01 × 10²⁴","6.02 × 10²⁴"],"D",3,0,"8677b0e49163"],["How many atoms in total are present in 1.5 mol of CH₄? (Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)",["4.515 × 10²⁴","3.01 × 10²⁴","9.03 × 10²³","4.515 × 10²⁵"],"A",3,0,"8677b0e49163"],["How many atoms in total are present in 3 mol of CH₄? (Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)",["3.01 × 10²⁴","9.03 × 10²⁵","1.806 × 10²⁴","9.03 × 10²⁴"],"D",3,0,"8677b0e49163"],["How many atoms in total are present in 5 mol of O₂? (Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)",["6.02 × 10²⁵","6.02 × 10²⁴","3.01 × 10²⁴","1.204 × 10²⁴"],"B",3,0,"8677b0e49163"],["How many moles of oxygen atoms are present in 0.5 mol of H₂SO₄?",["0.5","2","4","4.5"],"B",3,0,"8de74113c845"],["How many moles of hydrogen atoms are present in 2 mol of NH₃?",["6","2","12","3"],"A",3,0,"8de74113c845"],["How many moles of oxygen atoms are present in 0.5 mol of CO₂?",["1","0.5","2.5","2"],"A",3,0,"8de74113c845"],["How many moles of oxygen atoms are present in 0.25 mol of CO₂?",["2","0.5","1","0.25"],"B",3,0,"8de74113c845"],["How many moles of carbon atoms are present in 0.25 mol of C₆H₁₂O₆?",["6","3","1.5","0.25"],"C",3,0,"8de74113c845"],["How many moles of oxygen atoms are present in 2 mol of H₂SO₄?",["2","8","16","4"],"B",3,0,"8de74113c845"],["What volume does 1.5 mol of hydrogen gas occupy at STP? (molar volume = 22.4 dm³)",["0.067 dm³","33.6 dm³","22.4 dm³","14.93 dm³"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-gas-volume"],["What volume does 2 mol of hydrogen gas occupy at STP? (molar volume = 22.4 dm³)",["11.2 dm³","22.4 dm³","0.089 dm³","44.8 dm³"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-gas-volume"],["What volume does 0.25 mol of hydrogen gas occupy at STP? (molar volume = 22.4 dm³)",["0.011 dm³","89.6 dm³","5.6 dm³","22.4 dm³"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-gas-volume"],["What volume does 1.5 mol of oxygen gas occupy at STP? (molar volume = 22.4 dm³)",["33.6 dm³","0.067 dm³","22.4 dm³","14.93 dm³"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-gas-volume"],["What volume does 0.1 mol of nitrogen gas occupy at STP? (molar volume = 22.4 dm³)",["2.24 dm³","0.004 dm³","22.4 dm³","224 dm³"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-gas-volume"],["What volume does 3 mol of nitrogen gas occupy at STP? (molar volume = 22.4 dm³)",["0.134 dm³","22.4 dm³","67.2 dm³","7.47 dm³"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-gas-volume"],["What is the percentage by mass of S in SO₂? (S = 32, O = 16)",["32%","25%","33.3%","50%"],"D",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-percent-composition"],["What is the percentage by mass of O in H₂O? (H = 1, O = 16)",["88.9%","33.3%","11.1%","44.4%"],"A",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-percent-composition"],["What is the percentage by mass of O in CaCO₃? (Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)",["60%","16%","52%","48%"],"D",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-percent-composition"],["What is the percentage by mass of C in CO₂? (C = 12, O = 16)",["72.7%","33.3%","13.6%","27.3%"],"D",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-percent-composition"],["What is the percentage by mass of C in CH₄? (C = 12, H = 1)",["75%","25%","37.5%","20%"],"A",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-percent-composition"],["What is the percentage by mass of Mg in MgO? (Mg = 24, O = 16)",["60%","30%","50%","40%"],"A",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c6-percent-composition"],["A student calculates that a reaction should give 50 g of product but actually collects 47.5 g. The percentage yield is:",["5%","95%","105.3%","47.5%"],"B",3,1,"6996c937b647"],["A student calculates that a reaction should give 80 g of product but actually collects 64 g. The percentage yield is:",["80%","64%","20%","125%"],"A",3,1,"6996c937b647"],["A student calculates that a reaction should give 80 g of product but actually collects 48 g. The percentage yield is:",["60%","166.7%","48%","40%"],"A",3,1,"6996c937b647"],["A student calculates that a reaction should give 80 g of product but actually collects 76 g. The percentage yield is:",["5%","76%","105.3%","95%"],"D",3,1,"6996c937b647"],["A student calculates that a reaction should give 25 g of product but actually collects 22.5 g. The percentage yield is:",["111.1%","22.5%","90%","10%"],"C",3,1,"6996c937b647"],["A student calculates that a reaction should give 80 g of product but actually collects 60 g. The percentage yield is:",["60%","25%","75%","133.3%"],"C",3,1,"6996c937b647"],["A compound has the empirical formula CH. If its molar mass is 26 g/mol, its molecular formula is:",["C₂H₂","CH","C₃H₃","C₄H₄"],"A",5,0,"b60f5ea245a9"],["A compound has the empirical formula NO₂. If its molar mass is 92 g/mol, its molecular formula is:",["N₄O₈","NO₂","N₂O₄","N₃O₆"],"C",5,0,"b60f5ea245a9"],["A compound has the empirical formula CH₃. If its molar mass is 30 g/mol, its molecular formula is:",["C₄H₁₂","C₃H₉","C₂H₆","CH₃"],"C",5,0,"b60f5ea245a9"],["A compound has the empirical formula CH₂. If its molar mass is 70 g/mol, its molecular formula is:",["C₆H₁₂","C₄H₈","C₁₀H₂₀","C₅H₁₀"],"D",5,0,"b60f5ea245a9"],["A compound has the empirical formula CH₂. If its molar mass is 42 g/mol, its molecular formula is:",["C₃H₆","C₄H₈","C₆H₁₂","C₂H₄"],"A",5,0,"b60f5ea245a9"],["A compound has the empirical formula CH. If its molar mass is 78 g/mol, its molecular formula is:",["C₁₂H₁₂","C₅H₅","C₆H₆","C₇H₇"],"C",5,0,"b60f5ea245a9"]]$j$::jsonb, true);
